course: everyday
lesson: 199
title: Promises
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A promise is your word that you will do something. Keeping promises, even small ones, is how people learn they can rely on you. A pinky promise is still a promise.
explain: So only promise what you really mean to do. An empty «I promise I'll tidy later», said just to stop someone asking, teaches people your word means nothing.
explain: Sometimes you cannot keep a promise, because you are ill or plans change. Then tell the person as early as you can and say sorry. If you simply forgot, own up and make it right quickly.
explain: Some promises do not count: a promise forced by a threat, or one that controls who you can be friends with. You do not have to make them, and a promise made under threat should be told to an adult.
explain: Example: a girl in Colorado promised to look after a new puppy. She feeds it, gives it water and cleans up every day, even when she is busy, because the puppy depends on her.
fact: Only promise what you really mean to do.
fact: A pinky promise is still a promise, and people remember broken ones.
fact: Keeping small promises is how people learn they can rely on you.
fact: If you cannot keep a promise, tell the person as early as you can and say sorry.
fact: A promise to look after a pet means caring for it every day, because the animal depends on you.
fact: A borrowed thing should be returned on time and in the same condition.
fact: Grown-ups sometimes cannot keep promises because things change, and calmly talking about it helps.
fact: A promise made under threat does not count, and you should tell an adult about it.
fact: Dropping a promise for a better offer hurts the other person, so keep it or ask them honestly first.
fact: Empty promises teach others that your word means nothing.
fact: Younger children take promises very seriously.
fact: If you forget a promise, own up, say sorry and fix it as soon as you can.
fact: You do not have to make a promise that controls who you can be friends with.
exercise: A friend asks you to pinky promise. What should you remember? | Only promise what you really mean to do | Pinky promises do not really count at all | You can break it if you cross your fingers | It only counts if you shake hands on it too
exercise: You promised to watch a friend's game but now you are ill. What is right? | Tell him as early as you can and say sorry | Say nothing and hope he does not notice that you never came | Go anyway even though you are ill | Tell him you never promised anything
exercise: You promised to look after a new pet. What does that mean? | Feeding, watering and cleaning up after it every day | Playing with it whenever you feel like it and leaving the rest to others | Caring for it until you get bored | Letting your parents do it on busy days
exercise: You promised to return a borrowed book by Friday. What should you do? | Return it on time and in the same condition | Return it whenever you finish reading it, even if that is next month | Keep it, since it was only a small promise | Lend it to another friend first
exercise: Your parent promised a zoo trip but it was cancelled. What is a good reaction? | Say calmly how you feel and ask when you might go instead | Refuse to speak to them for a week | Decide that grown-ups never keep their promises, so stop believing them | Say they lied to you on purpose
exercise: An older child threatens you to make you promise to keep a secret. What should you do? | Tell an adult, because a promise under threat does not count | Keep the promise, because a promise is a promise, whatever happens | Make him promise something back | Keep it only until the end of the week
exercise: You promised to play with one friend, then another invites you somewhere more exciting. What is right? | Keep your first promise or ask the first friend honestly | Go to the exciting place without telling anyone | Tell the first friend you are ill | Bring the first friend along to the exciting place without asking anyone
exercise: You said «I promise I'll tidy my room later» just to stop being asked. What should you do? | Do it, or do not promise in the first place | Forget it, since it was just to stop the nagging | Tidy it only if someone checks on you later | Promise again the next time that they ask you
exercise: You promised your little brother you would help him build a model. Why keep it? | Younger children take promises very seriously | Because models are hard to build alone and he would get stuck | Because he will tell your parents | Because small promises do not matter
exercise: You completely forgot a promise. What is best? | Own up, say sorry and fix it as soon as you can | Pretend you never made the promise | Blame someone else for reminding you late | Wait and hope the other person forgets about it too, so nobody is upset
exercise: A friend says «Promise you'll never be friends with her.» What is true? | You do not have to make that kind of promise | You must promise, or else you are not being a good friend to her | Promise and then break it secretly | Promise, but only for one week
exercise: Why does keeping small promises matter? | It is how people learn they can rely on you | Small promises never matter to anyone, so they can be forgotten | Only big promises build trust | It is only important with grown-ups
test: A boy in Arizona tells his mum every day «I promise I'll practise piano later» and never does. What is happening to his promises? | They are starting to mean nothing to her | They are getting stronger and stronger each time | She will believe him a little more each day | Promises that are repeated always come true
test: A girl in Halifax is sick on the day she promised to help at the bake sale. Which choice is best? | Message the organiser early to say she cannot come and say sorry | Not tell anyone, since being ill is not her fault | Go anyway, even though she has a fever | Ask her little sister to go instead and not tell anyone about the swap
test: Which promise does NOT have to be kept? | One you were forced to make by someone threatening you | One you made to your grandmother last week about visiting her soon | A pinky promise made to a good friend | A promise to return a library book
test: You promised to go swimming with Sam, then Ava invites you to a theme park on the same day. What is fair? | Keep your plan with Sam, or ask him honestly if you may change it | Go to the theme park and tell Sam you were ill, so his feelings are not hurt | Go to the theme park and say nothing to Sam | Ask Ava to invite Sam instead of you
test: Why does a pet promise matter more than many other promises? | An animal depends on you for food, water and care | Pets remember every single promise you make and get sad if you break one | Pets are more expensive than toys | Your parents will take the pet away
test: Your dad promised a bike ride, then his work called him in. What would help most? | Telling him calmly you are disappointed and asking for another day | Saying loudly that he always breaks every promise he ever makes to you | Going on the bike ride alone instead | Deciding never to believe him again
test: A girl forgot she had promised to feed her neighbour's cat. What should she do? | Feed it now, tell the neighbour and say sorry | Hope the cat found some food by itself | Say the neighbour never asked her | Leave extra food tomorrow and not mention it to the neighbour at all
test: A boy borrows a friend's comic and spills juice on it. What is honest? | Tell his friend and offer to replace it | Return it quietly and hope he does not notice | Keep it and say that he lost it on the bus | Tell him it was already like that when he got it
test: promised to play w my sis but now my mate's invited me to the cinema | Keep the promise or ask your sister honestly first | Just go, she'll get over it | Tell her you're ill | Take your mate's offer and say nothing, she won't even notice you've gone
test: kid said promise ur not friends with Ella or else | That promise doesn't count; tell an adult | You must keep it now | Promise and stop seeing Ella | Ask Ella to promise too
test: borrowed jo's book, i got jam on it, oops | Own up and offer to fix or replace it | Give it back and say nothing, she may not even see the jam | Keep it so she forgets | Say it was like that already
test: dad promised the zoo but now he's gotta work | Talk calmly about it, things change sometimes | Scream and shout at him until he gives in and goes | Say he never keeps any of his promises, so he owes you | Never trust him again about anything he promises
explain: A boy in Ohio promised to keep his little brother safe at the playground and then went off with friends; his brother wandered to the road and a neighbour brought him back.
explain: A girl in Leeds kept promising to return a friend's book and never did; the friend stopped lending her things.
fact: Breaking a promise to keep someone safe can put them in danger.
fact: People stop trusting someone who often breaks promises.
fact: A promise to keep a secret does not count if someone is being hurt; tell a trusted adult.
test: promised to watch lil bro at the park but my mates are calling | Keep your promise and stay with him | Go, he'll be ok | Tell him to follow you | Ask a stranger at the park to watch him while you play
test: keep promising to give back his game but forgot again | Give it back today, broken promises lose trust | Keep it, he'll forget | Say you lost it | Promise again tomorrow, and this time say it more seriously
test: made a promise to keep a secret but it's someone getting hurt | Tell a trusted adult, safety beats the promise | Keep it, a promise is a promise, whatever it is about | Tell only the person who did it, so they know you know | Write it in your diary only and tell no one else
test: did u hear Jack promised to watch his little bro at the park but went off with mates n the kid got all the way to the motorway!! | A promise to keep someone safe must be kept; "motorway" may be exaggerated | Promises to little brothers and sisters don't count as much as ones to friends | Little kids never wander off | Leaving is fine if mates ask
test: apparently Ella made Grace pinky promise not to be friends with anyone else, n now Grace is crying, spread it | Don't spread it; a promise controlling your friends doesn't count, tell an adult | Spread it so everyone knows | Grace must keep the promise | Make Ella pinky promise too
test: my mate says Harry borrowed a game 3 months ago n "lost" it, he always does that, he's a thief | It's unkind to call him a thief; the lesson is to return borrowed things on time | Yes, call him a thief at school | Borrowed things never need returning | Steal something of his back
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a pinky promise? | It is still a promise, and broken ones are remembered | It is a game and need not be kept by anyone | It is valid only between children of the same age | It counts only when an adult has witnessed it
exam: It is given that one is unable to keep a promise owing to illness. Determine the appropriate course of action. | Tell the person as early as possible and say sorry | Say nothing and hope the person forgets about it | Make a new promise without mentioning the old one | Blame someone else for the promise not being kept
exam: Identify the status of a promise made under threat. | It does not count, and an adult should be told | It must be kept, since a promise is a promise | It counts only if it was a pinky promise | It counts only until the next school day
exam: Select the response consistent with established facts concerning a promise to look after a pet. | It means caring for the animal every day, since it depends on one | It means caring for the animal only when one is not busy | It means caring for the animal on weekends and holidays | It may be dropped once the animal has grown up
